Example usage (JS):

```javascript
import { CookieJar } from 'tough-cookie';

const cookieJar = new CookieJar();
cookieJar.setCookieSync('preset-cookie=123; Path=/', 'https://example.com');

const impit = new Impit({ cookieJar });
// impit finds the set cookie and sends it with the request.
const response = await impit.fetch('https://example.com'); 
//If the response above contains `Set-Cookie` headers, impit stores them in the `cookieJar` (and they will be immediately available from JS).
```

Hello, and thank you for your interest in this project.

I believe this is correct behaviour of `impit`. When reusing the same `Impit` instance, it will try to reuse the same TCP connection for multiple HTTP requests (to the same server). This is to save time and network bandwidth when requesting multiple resources from the same server (often, browsers do this when loading HTML, CSS, images etc. all at once).

It's possible that all those 10 requests in your demo are made using the same TCP connection, using the same HTTP tunnel through the proxy servers. 

This might be the difference between using `impit` and testing the "same" scenario using e.g. 10 separate `curl` calls.

If my theory is correct, creating a new `Impit` instance for each request should make the proxies rotate the IP addresses.

