Hello and thank you for your interest in this project!

The library shouldn't memleak on this example, so that's definitely something for us to look into.

If I understand your request correctly, you might use a custom `CookieJar` implementation. `Impit` accepts any object that implements the `getCookieSync` and `setCookie` methods, so you can e.g. create a `FreezableCookieJar` class like this:

```javascript
import { Impit } from 'impit';
import { CookieJar } from 'tough-cookie';

class FreezableCookieJar extends CookieJar {
    constructor(...args) {
        super(...args);
        this.frozen = false;
    }

    freeze() {
        this.frozen = true;
    }

    unfreeze() {
        this.frozen = false;
    }

    setCookie(...args) {
        if (this.frozen) return;
        return super.setCookie(...args);
    }
}

const cj = new FreezableCookieJar();

const impit = new Impit({
    cookieJar: cj,
})

await impit.fetch('https://example.com'); // sets cookies

cj.freeze(); // disables the setCookie method

// subsequent requests won't store any new cookies
await impit.fetch('https://example.com/2');
await impit.fetch('https://example.com/3');
await impit.fetch('https://example.com/4');
await impit.fetch('https://example.com/5');
```

A similar implementation can be used to e.g. only let the clients to set some cookie names, preset some cookies manually etc.

As I mentioned above, this is only a workaround, though. We'll continue looking into the memory leak.

