I've made a small investigation on this, and it looks like a non-trivial task.

Investigation Summary
--

The root cause is that hyper-util's tunnel implementation discards the proxy's HTTP status code. In tunnel.rs, the CONNECT response is parsed, but the status code is thrown away:

- HTTP/1.1 200 - success
- HTTP/1.1 407 - TunnelError::ProxyAuthRequired (no status code stored)
- Anything else - TunnelError::TunnelUnsuccessful (unit variant, zero data)

Additionally, TunnelError is a private type (not re-exported), so downstream crates can't even downcast to inspect it. The error eventually surfaces in Node.js as a generic Error with a debug-formatted message string.

Proposed Solution
--

Three layers of changes:

1. Fork hyper-util - carry the status code

    Patch TunnelError::TunnelUnsuccessful to TunnelUnsuccessful(u16) so it preserves the proxy's response status code. This is a ~10-line change in tunnel.rs. We already maintain forks of h2, rustls, and tower-http.

2. Thread the status code through Rust - napi boundary

